WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.720
[Narrator:] This is one of the most important buildings in any community, its hospital.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.660
Within these walls a variety of medical services are performed.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.500
Ever-expanding, ever-changing in application and scope.

00:00:13.300 --> 00:00:16.740
Responsible for care during so many phases of life.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:25.740
It's bringing forth, it's preservation, and continued good health into later years.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:31.460
The modern hospital faces many problems in caring for the population of a growing community

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:32.860
and a growing nation.

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:37.600
To help foster better hospital facilities and better, more efficient care,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:42.000
many hospital authorities and members of the medical and nursing professions

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.260
are applying a concept known as: progressive patient care.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.360
In this film we shall explore a major element of this concept, the element of intensive care.

00:00:54.760 --> 00:01:12.300
[Music]

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:15.340
[John Shaffer:] Hello, I'm John Shaffer.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:21.540
As the administrator of a hospital which has established a program of progressive patient care,

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:27.380
I've been asked to tell you something about it, particularly about one phase of it, intensive care.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:33.600
I've asked Miss Carolyn Jones, who is head nurse of our 27-bed intensive care unit, to assist me.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:40.260
We've chosen to discuss intensive care because in the planning and implementation of a complete,

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:46.280
or even a partial program of progressive patient care, the setting up of an intensive care unit

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.560
represents a major consideration and a logical first step.

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:57.520
To begin with, let's review briefly all the elements of progressive patient care

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:01.180
to see where intensive care fits into the overall concept.

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.160
Generally, progressive patient care,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:10.380
which groups patients according to their care needs, is composed of six elements.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:18.040
For the critically or seriously ill patient who needs constant observation and highly skilled nursing care,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:19.720
there is intensive care.

00:02:21.880 --> 00:02:25.320
For those patients who are moderately ill, on their way to recovery,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.700
or terminally ill and requiring palliative treatment, 
there are the services of intermediate care.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:38.440
Ambulatory patients who are convalescing, and those requiring only diagnosis and therapy,

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:40.680
can be treated in a self-care unit.

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.260
To care for those who may be hospitalized for prolonged periods,

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:52.600
such as patients requiring extensive rehabilitation and physical therapy, there is long-term care.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:58.460
Hospital-administered services are extended to a patient's own home,

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:02.160
with the help of other health organizations, by a home care unit.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.560
And finally, for those patients who visit the hospital only for diagnosis and treatment,

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:12.180
and do not require a bed, there is outpatient care.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:18.360
These are the basic elements of a complete progressive patient care program,

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:23.780
And, depending on the hospital and the individual community's resources and needs,

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.960
a progressive patient care program can be composed of all these elements,

00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.160
or perhaps just two or three of them.

00:03:32.240 --> 00:03:34.560
Now let's take a closer look at intensive care,

00:03:35.260 --> 00:03:38.660
in terms of the planning that must go into the setting up of such a unit,

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:42.420
its physical requirements and its staffing and administration.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:49.040
The purpose of intensive care is to place critically and seriously ill patients

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:51.520
in an area where they can be under constant observation,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.980
where they can receive immediate attention for any emergency.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:59.540
Patients here may include severe burns or multiple fractures,

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.140
major gastrointestinal hemorrhages,

00:04:28.240 --> 00:04:31.540
major surgical cases just released from the recovery room

00:04:31.540 --> 00:04:35.260
will require continued observation after the post-anesthesia phase.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.860
Patients with critical injuries

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:44.320
or acute coronaries.

00:04:46.540 --> 00:04:50.920
In addition to the seriousness of the patient's condition, his clinical diagnosis,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:57.400
an important criterion for placing him under intensive care is the nature of the nursing care required.

00:04:58.680 --> 00:05:02.000
Those requiring the most constant attention would be placed here.

00:05:03.980 --> 00:05:08.820
How does a hospital interested in establishing an intensive care unit begin its planning?

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:12.900
This is one way to do it. This is the way we did it.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:19.520
A preliminary step was the designation of a patient evaluation team.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.880
This team included the hospital administrator and selected members of the medical and nursing staffs.

00:05:25.480 --> 00:05:30.520
We had the responsibility of evaluating the care requirements of all the patients in the hospital.

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:37.060
To do this, we developed a checklist of patients' needs to use as a guide in determining care requirements.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:43.180
This checklist is based on the patient's need for observation, physical assistance,

00:05:43.680 --> 00:05:46.340
and special medical or nursing skills and equipment.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:53.340
Although this list is by no means exhaustive, it was possible for us to classify our patients fairly well.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:05:57.920
For example, under need for observation,

00:05:58.260 --> 00:06:02.440
we included the frequency of checking temperature, pulse, respiration,

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:05.200
blood pressure and degree of consciousness.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:11.980
The need for physical assistance included bathing, mobility, and need for assistance with meals.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:18.780
Special medical or nursing skills and the use of special equipment is involved when oxygen therapy,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.220
transfusion, or infusion are called for,

00:06:21.700 --> 00:06:24.720
or when aspiration of a tracheostomy tube is needed.

00:06:27.820 --> 00:06:34.540
With the help of this checklist, our patient evaluation team next determined criteria for patient classification,

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:40.240
classification which allowed us to determine the most appropriate care unit for each patient.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:49.420
Having established criteria for patient classification, the patient population was observed daily for three months,

00:06:49.760 --> 00:06:52.940
and an average census established in terms of care need.

00:06:53.680 --> 00:06:55.600
This census was the determining factor

00:06:56.060 --> 00:07:00.820
in estimating the average number of beds which our hospital would need for intensive care.

00:07:01.260 --> 00:07:08.740
On some days the census indicated a need for 26 beds, and on other days the census was as low as 10.

00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:16.120
We decided from this that we would need a basic intensive care unit of 17 beds.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.820
From the study estimating bed needs by the Public Health Service,

00:07:21.260 --> 00:07:24.320
we found that for our average daily census of 19,

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:29.520
we would need about 10 additional beds to provide for fluctuation in census.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:36.680
This model shows the arrangement of our intermediate and intensive care units,

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:43.480
and on it you can see how our intensive care unit provides for our census fluctuation.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:52.420
The intensive care unit consists of the larger intensive care rooms and several adjacent two-bed rooms.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:58.600
These two-bed rooms are so equipped and located that with proper staffing

00:07:58.600 --> 00:08:03.440
they can be used for either intensive care or intermediate care patients.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:07.340
We refer to these rooms as our flexible zone.

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:09.380
Our intensive care rooms,

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:14.520
plus the additional beds, form a patient care unit under the direction of a head nurse.

00:08:15.180 --> 00:08:20.300
Should our need for intensive care beds increase, additional beds can be provided,

00:08:20.400 --> 00:08:24.900
because our intensive care unit is adjacent to an intermediate care unit.

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:31.140
Having seen briefly how we establish the bed needs of our intensive care unit,

00:08:31.620 --> 00:08:34.400
let's look for a moment at an intensive care room.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:42.900
As I stated before, one of the purposes of intensive care is to place critically and seriously ill patients

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:45.700
in an area where they can be under constant observation.

00:08:46.320 --> 00:08:52.700
We found that a room layout was required in which the nurse or nurses on duty could see and hear

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:58.740
all patients at all times, and in which they can reach each patient quickly when necessary.

00:08:59.180 --> 00:09:02.220
Also important is ample space between beds

00:09:02.680 --> 00:09:07.700
to allow the nursing team to work comfortably with any equipment necessary at the bedside.

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:13.920
One such layout is this six-bed room, which we adopted.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:19.540
In this arrangement, the nurse can observe all patients from the desk or any other point in the room.

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:24.120
An alternative to the six-bed room is our five-bed arrangement.

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:30.800
In this room the beds are separated by partitions, with glass from 40 inches above the floor to the ceiling.

00:09:31.280 --> 00:09:37.340
The partitions provide some degree of privacy but still do not obstruct the nurses' view of all patients.

00:09:39.420 --> 00:09:43.860
Rooms with fewer beds have been adapted to intensive care use in some hospitals,

00:09:44.700 --> 00:09:49.180
but the principle that a nurse be on duty in the room at all times must be adhered to.

00:09:50.660 --> 00:09:53.860
We have found the five and six-bed rooms

00:09:53.860 --> 00:09:57.840
to be the most economical and efficient arrangements for intensive care rooms,

00:09:58.240 --> 00:10:01.760
particularly from the standpoint of the nurse-to-patient ratio.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.280
These are some room layouts and bed arrangements for intensive care.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:11.820
Equally important to the size and type of room is the equipment.

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:16.360
Intensive care calls for the availability of much special equipment

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:18.980
and a highly skilled nursing staff to use it.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.620
Miss Jones, you helped plan and set up our intensive care unit.

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:24.400
Would you tell us a little more about it?

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:29.640
[Nurse Jones:] First, let's take a look at the equipment in one of our intensive care rooms.

00:10:31.660 --> 00:10:36.640
In this six-bed room much of the equipment is immediately accessible at each bedside.

00:10:37.280 --> 00:10:44.180
For example, at every bedside there is an oxygen needle valve outlet with flowmeter and humidifier attached.

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:50.660
Suction outlets with a regulator, pressure gauge, and vacuum bottle are all at hand.

00:10:51.700 --> 00:10:57.060
Sufficient electrical outlets with the proper voltages are present to handle portable equipment.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.440
Also available over each bed are intravenous rods.

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.520
Wall-mounted sphygmomanometers are at each bedside,

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:15.620
and a call outlet for the nurses' use in an emergency.

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.540
Medications and emergency drugs are kept in the medication cabinet.

00:11:22.300 --> 00:11:26.340
Equipment and supplies not at bedsides are readily available in the room.

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:33.600
Necessary supplies are on mobile carts

00:11:33.600 --> 00:11:37.660
and include such equipment as might be needed to perform emergency procedures.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:46.360
Clean linens are kept on a cart in the storage room.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:53.680
Special equipment such as a pacemaker and a defibrillator may also be kept in the intensive care room.

00:11:55.000 --> 00:11:58.360
Any needed items which are not at hand can be ordered quickly

00:11:58.360 --> 00:12:02.580
by telephone or intercom from the nurses' station or the supply area,

00:12:04.720 --> 00:12:10.940
but most important to intensive care patients is a well-trained, highly skilled nursing staff.

00:12:11.980 --> 00:12:17.760
We had a two-week orientation for our staff on the policies and procedures for the intensive care unit,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:24.820
and have a continuing in-service program on special procedures, use of new equipment, new medications,

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:27.320
and care of patients with special problems.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:33.540
Nurses responsible for intensive care must have the technical skills

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:36.300
to provide a variety of special nursing services.

00:12:36.940 --> 00:12:41.680
They must also be suited in temperament and personality to work efficiently under pressure

00:12:41.960 --> 00:12:44.660
in dealing with many emergency situations.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:51.840
Adequate staffing 24 hours a day is necessary in an intensive care unit.

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.660
The size of the staff may vary according to the number of patients,

00:12:56.100 --> 00:13:01.040
but at least one professional nurse is present in our intensive care room at all times.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:07.720
A graduate nurse and a practical nurse or a nurse's aide are usually required in a five- or six-bed room,

00:13:07.940 --> 00:13:12.960
which compares with a nurse and an aide to 12 to 14 patients on intermediate care.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:18.060
Sometimes one or more additional nurses may be needed to care

00:13:18.060 --> 00:13:21.140
for the very sick patients requiring full-time attention.

00:13:21.860 --> 00:13:25.960
The nurses in our unit ask to work there and have found the work quite rewarding.

00:13:26.520 --> 00:13:29.380
We have several other nurses who are prepared to work in the unit

00:13:29.380 --> 00:13:34.640
and are called on to fill in during vacations, illnesses, or as permanent replacements.

00:13:37.100 --> 00:13:41.040
We come now to the operations aspect of an intensive care unit.

00:13:41.600 --> 00:13:48.360
Our intensive care unit admits both medical and surgical patients, some directly from the recovery room,

00:13:48.420 --> 00:13:52.140
some from the emergency room, and some from other patient units.

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:58.240
In many respects, its administration is similar to other units, but there are some exceptions.

00:13:58.760 --> 00:14:04.920
In the case of admissions, while many patients will be admitted to intensive care through standard procedure,

00:14:05.300 --> 00:14:11.040
a system for direct admission of emergency cases has been arranged in this hospital.

00:14:13.280 --> 00:14:18.720
This is accomplished by allowing the physician to call directly to the head nurse of the intensive care unit

00:14:18.720 --> 00:14:20.520
to see whether a bed is available.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:25.160
If not, we have a special procedure to follow in arranging for a bed.

00:14:25.900 --> 00:14:31.900
Through his call, the doctor then arranges for a bed, orders medications, intravenous fluids if necessary,

00:14:32.220 --> 00:14:35.920
and equipment to be available immediately upon the patient's arrival.

00:14:42.040 --> 00:14:48.280
The time saved by this direct admissions procedure has been vital to several critically ill patients.

00:15:14.020 --> 00:15:19.480
Another procedure useful in our intensive care is the keeping of a special record form for each patient.

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:26.300
The form covers a 24-hour period and includes temperature, pulse, respiration and blood pressure

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:32.440
taken every two hours or as often as ordered, as well as measurements of fluid intake and output.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:40.420
Although intensive care patients are allowed visitors, we limit visits to about five minutes on the hour.

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:44.740
This has proved satisfactory to our patients and most visitors.

00:15:47.020 --> 00:15:49.560
A family waiting room is located near the unit

00:15:49.560 --> 00:15:53.620
to permit relatives to be close to the patient without interfering with his treatment.

00:15:55.260 --> 00:16:00.920
Since the dietary needs of patients in intensive care vary greatly and change frequently,

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:04.600
a flexible dietary service is especially necessary.

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:11.620
In order to give the intensive care nurse every opportunity to devote her full attention to her patients,

00:16:12.080 --> 00:16:14.580
certain services are made available to her.

00:16:15.900 --> 00:16:21.000
Soiled and used equipment is collected by an aide or orderly at frequent intervals.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:27.780
Supplies are replaced and replenished regularly according to a prearranged schedule.

00:16:28.380 --> 00:16:32.520
This relieves the nurse of having to check inventory and prepare requisitions.

00:16:35.100 --> 00:16:36.080
[John Shaffer:] Thank you, Miss Jones.

00:16:38.500 --> 00:16:42.380
We have shown you some of the requirements and the kinds of services

00:16:42.760 --> 00:16:46.060
which add to the efficient operation of the intensive care unit,

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:49.300
a major element of progressive patient care.

00:16:51.160 --> 00:16:53.560
The setting-up of such a unit has many requirements.

00:16:54.880 --> 00:16:57.820
Careful planning and teamwork are required at the outset.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:01.720
A thorough evaluation of the hospital's needs should be made.

00:17:03.640 --> 00:17:06.440
Flexibility should be provided for in all plans.

00:17:07.480 --> 00:17:12.740
A highly skilled staff must be oriented to new methods, trained in new procedures.

00:17:13.400 --> 00:17:16.480
Definite administrative policies must be established,

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:20.960
and patients and public informed about the operations of the unit.

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:27.620
We have found that our intensive care unit meets the needs of our critically ill patients,

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:35.000
assures the doctor of close and careful observation of his patients and makes better use of personnel.

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:42.000
This unit was our first step toward a more complete program of progressive patient care.

00:17:44.200 --> 00:17:53.140
[Music]

00:18:28.600 --> 00:18:31.733
[Technical Advisors: Charles F. Wagner, Josephine Strachan]

00:18:31.733 --> 00:18:34.733
[Directed by: Jack C. Kirkland]

00:18:34.733 --> 00:18:40.166
[THE END]

